// TransferValidity: proves that a single balance transfer is valid without
// revealing the transferred amount.
//
// Private witness:
//   amount - amount transferred
//
// Public inputs:
//   senderBalanceBefore    - sender's balance before the transfer
//   senderBalanceAfter     - sender's balance after the transfer
//   recipientBalanceBefore - recipient's balance before the transfer
//   recipientBalanceAfter  - recipient's balance after the transfer
//
// Constraints enforced:
//   - the sender has enough balance to cover the transfer (no underflow)
//   - the sender's new balance is exactly balance - amount
//   - the recipient's new balance is exactly balance + amount
//     (i.e. value is conserved: nothing is created or destroyed)
//
// Balances are public (not just the amount) so an on-chain settlement
// contract (chain/settlement) can check the proof's claimed "before"
// balances against its own stored ledger before applying the "after"
// balances — otherwise it would have no way to tell a proof of a real
// transfer from a proof of a transfer starting from a made-up balance.
// An earlier version of this circuit kept senderBalanceBefore private,
// which looked more private but was actually unusable for settlement:
// see chain/settlement/README.md.
//
// Balances use `field` rather than a fixed-width uint: HYDRO has 18
// decimals, so even a modest balance like 1000 HYDRO is 1000 * 10^18 base
// units — far past u64's ~1.8*10^19 ceiling (about 18 whole HYDRO). An
// earlier version of this circuit used u64, which "worked" only for
// unrealistically small test balances; it couldn't have represented a
// real holder's balance once wired to actual HYDRO (chain/settlement's
// deposit/withdraw). `field` is ZoKrates' native ~254-bit prime field
// element — comfortably larger than the entire 371M-HYDRO max supply
// (~2^88 base units) — so comparisons and arithmetic here are exact, not
// an approximation, for any real HYDRO amount.
//
// This is the smallest building block of a rollup validity proof, not a
// full batch/state-transition circuit for the whole chain — see
// zk/circuits/README.md for scope and what's still missing.
def main(field senderBalanceBefore, private field amount, field senderBalanceAfter, field recipientBalanceBefore, field recipientBalanceAfter) -> bool {
    assert(amount <= senderBalanceBefore);
    assert(senderBalanceAfter == senderBalanceBefore - amount);
    assert(recipientBalanceAfter == recipientBalanceBefore + amount);
    return true;
}
